---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion, nodes } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const raid = assertion('the-night-the-jail-was-taken')
const fort = assertion('the-county-is-settled-from-outside-itself')
const register = assertion('sixteen-of-twenty-nine-are-one-days-work')
const warLists = assertion('the-two-war-lists-do-not-join')
const succession = assertion('the-office-passed-from-father-to-son')
const bank = assertion('the-man-in-the-cell-was-charged-with-an-allen-county-bank')
const tanks = assertion('the-county-had-two-tank-installations')
const books = assertion('the-war-turned-the-locomotive-works-books')
const railTown = assertion('lima-was-a-railroad-town-before-an-oil-town')
const noTrains = assertion('no-passenger-train-calls-in-this-county')
const wentToWar = assertion('one-person-in-ten-went-to-war')
const warDead = assertion('the-roster-never-totalled-its-own-dead')
const corinth = assertion('more-died-at-corinth-than-anywhere')
const trains = assertion('the-page-adds-up-and-the-forecast-does-not')

const booksSpec = bar(books.figures.map((f) => f.label), books.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Locomotives ordered',
  labelValues: true,
})

const warListSpec = bar(
  warLists.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  warLists.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Allen County men in the World War', labelValues: true },
)

const causeSpec = bar(
  warDead.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  warDead.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Men (a floor)', labelValues: true },
)

const corinthSpec = bar(
  corinth.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  corinth.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Men (a floor)', labelValues: true },
)

const trainsSpec = bar(
  trains.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  trains.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Passenger trains a day into Lima', labelValues: true },
)

const crossingSpec = bar(
  noTrains.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  noTrains.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Open crossings', labelValues: true },
)

// The spine is read out of the graph, never typed here. A page that lists the county's dated
// record by hand would keep saying "1885" long after a phase had added something later, and the
// gap this page is largely about would be the first thing to go stale.
const year = (s: string | undefined) => (s ? Number(s.slice(0, 4)) : Number.NaN)

const events = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'event' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.occurred)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, at: year(n.properties.occurred), on: n.properties.occurred }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.at - b.at)

const periods = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.class === 'period' && !Number.isNaN(year(n.properties.began)))
  .map((n) => ({ node: n, from: year(n.properties.began), to: year(n.properties.ended) }))
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.from - b.from)

// The longest run of years with no dated event in it, computed from the same list the table
// renders. Written as a pair so the prose below can name both ends without repeating itself.
const gaps = events.slice(1).map((e, i) => ({ from: events[i].at, to: e.at }))
const widest = gaps.reduce((a, b) => (b.to - b.from > a.to - a.from ? b : a), gaps[0])
const last = events[events.length - 1].at
const since = new Date().getUTCFullYear() - last
---

<Base
  title="History"
  lede="Everything this corpus can date, in order — and the stretches between."
>
  <section>
    <h2>The county's dated record, and the hole in the middle of it</h2>
    <p>
      A civic reference should be able to say when things happened. This one can say it {
        events.length
      } times. The list below is not a history of Allen County; it is the subset of the county's
      history that this corpus has put a date on from a source it can cite, which is a much
      smaller thing and is shown here at its real size.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Dated events in the corpus, earliest first</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Kind</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          events.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.on}</td>
              <td>{e.node.label}</td>
              <td>{e.node.properties.event_type ?? '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The gaps are the point. The widest run with nothing dated in it is {widest.to - widest.from}
      years, from {widest.from} to {widest.to}; the longest of all is the {since} years since {last},
      which is where this corpus stops being able to name a day at all. That is not because nothing
      happened. It is because the corpus spent most of its phases counting rather than dating, and a
      count has no date on it — it has a year and a table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Between the oil strike and the raid the same pattern holds in reverse. The corpus can tell you
      that Lima's manufacturing workforce grew 82 per cent between 1899 and 1909 and that the county
      reached 56,580 people in 1910, and it cannot tell you the day either thing happened, because
      neither is the kind of thing that has one. What it has known across those years is what Allen
      County <em>was</em>. What happened in them is newer work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The most recent entry came from a land record rather than from a newspaper, and that is worth
      noticing about this list. A conveyance is dated to the day whether or not anybody reported it,
      and the county has abstracted its conveyances by piece of ground since the federal patents. A
      corpus that wants to know when things happened here has been reading the wrong kind of source.
    </p>
    <p>
      One entry on this list was added because the list existed. The 1918 renaming of German
      Township had been established two phases earlier, with the day and the petition's own
      language, and recorded as prose on a place and on a question — which is where an answer goes
      and not where a happening goes. It was missing from every view built on the event class, and
      the stretch after 1885 was reading fifteen years emptier than the corpus's own knowledge.
      Publishing found it; reading had not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The named spans do reach across it, because a period is a claim about a stretch rather than
      about a day:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Named periods in the corpus</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Span</th><th scope="col">Period</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          periods.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.from}–{Number.isNaN(p.to) ? 'present' : p.to}</td>
              <td>{p.node.label}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The fort across the line</h2>
    <p>
      The county's dated record now opens in September 1812, with a stockade on the west bank of
      the Auglaize: an acre and a half inside eleven-foot pickets set four feet into the ground,
      four corner blockhouses with their upper storeys overhanging, a storehouse in the middle.
      Colonel Thomas Poague's command built it after cutting the road from St. Marys, where
      Harrison had his headquarters, to Fort Defiance, and Poague named it for his wife Amanda.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={fort} />
    <p>
      Three county histories place Fort Amanda in Allen County. The 1885 book opens its settlement
      chapter with <em>the initial point in the history of the settlement of Allen County must be
      known as Fort Amanda</em>, and calls it the county's first post office and its first place of
      religious meeting. The 1906 book reprints that. The 1921 book knows better and argues with it
      in the same paragraph — <em>it was a rude fate that removed Fort Amanda with its historic
      traditions from the bounds of Allen County</em> — before settling the matter with a verse:
      <em>Prove all things and hold fast that which is good, and in its traditions Fort Amanda
      belongs to Allen County.</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      The federal gazetteer puts it in Auglaize County. So does the county's own map service, by
      declining to answer: the fort's coordinate returns nothing from the township layer, nothing
      from the survey sections and nothing from the parcels, where a control point a mile north
      returns <code>AMANDA TOWNSHIP</code> from the first of them. Walking north along the same
      meridian until the service begins to answer puts the line at latitude 40.685776 — 0.188
      statute miles, about nine hundred and ninety feet, from the fort. The 1848 act that made
      Auglaize County kept <em>the north half of Amanda</em> and let the south half go with the
      fort in it, so the township still carries the name and no longer contains what it is named
      for.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the fort did was carry freight. The garrison spent the winter of 1812–13 as a
      shipbuilding company — one detail choosing trees, one felling, one sawing, one building flat
      boats — and shipped flour downstream in barrels. The single contemporary document anyone
      quotes is Green Clay's letter of 20 June 1813 from Camp Meigs, recording a consignment
      received from Fort Amanda through Ensign Gray. The 1921 history calls that <em>an utter
      impossibility on the Auglaize today</em> and gives the reason: the timber gone and the
      country tiled, so the water runs off instead of standing. A river was made unnavigable by
      clearing the ground around it.
    </p>
    <p>
      There was never a battle here. The one death the sources record is Captain Enoch Dawson,
      shot from ambush in October 1812 while eating grapes at the water's edge. Four years after
      the garrison left, three men moved their families into the blockhouses — Andrew Russell,
      Peter Diltz and William Van Ausdall — and two of the three are buried in the military
      cemetery beside the soldiers, which is not what a national cemetery is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything counted there comes out at seventy-five. Seventy-five mounds in 1885, some 75
      graves in 1906, close to seventy-five in 1915 with only about forty carrying a marker — and
      seventy-five flat boats built that first winter, on the facing subject a few pages earlier in
      the same book. The identities went when the British burned the Capitol and the garrison's
      records with it. And the 1906 history is not a third witness: its Fort Amanda chapter
      reprints the 1885 text sentence for sentence, the same Harrison date and the same Ensign
      Gray, which leaves two witnesses where the shelf appears to hold three.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The other war, and the list that outran the count</h2>
    <p>
      The same chapter of the same book that gave this site the Civil War gives the First World
      War, and this site had never read it. Chapter XLIV of the 1921 county history, "Allen County
      in the Wars", runs fifty pages and ends in two rosters.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={warLists} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="warlists"
      title="How many Allen County men were in the World War"
      caption="Three answers from one chapter. The first two are the American Legion's, reported by its Ohio commander; the third is a count of the names actually printed in the roster twenty pages later, and it is a floor — ninety-six further lines are visibly names the scan mangled."
      columns={['Count', 'Men']}
      rows={warLists.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="warlists" set:html={JSON.stringify(warListSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The roster belongs to Elmer McClain, an Allen County man who enlisted, did transport duty and
      kept a card system. He names twelve sources for it — ex-service men, the YMCA at the camps,
      the American Legion, the Federal Employment Bureau, the county recorder's office, the 1919
      Lima city directory, the township chairmen of the Victory Loan campaign, the Knights of
      Columbus, the Red Cross, the draft board, the historical society — and he says what it is
      worth: <em>the compiler knows that it is not in every respect either perfectly complete or
      perfectly accurate, but it is the most complete and accurate list in existence.</em> The book
      cannot spell him. He is Elmert in the paragraph that introduces the list and Elmer in the line
      above the first name.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ninety-five men are on the honour roll that follows, and the two lists do not join. Matched on
      surname and first initial, at most forty-seven of the ninety-five dead are missing from the
      roster of the served — and one of the missing is William Paul Gallagher. The service roster is
      alphabetical and the slot where he belongs runs Galberach, Gallant, Gallapool, Gallaspie,
      Gallont, Galloway: a continuous sequence with no Gallagher in it, which is what a name that
      was never entered looks like rather than one the scan lost. The American Legion post in Lima
      is the William Paul Gallagher Post No. 96, and it helped compile the honour roll.
    </p>
    <p>
      Against the county's other war the shape is inverted. One person in sixteen served, against
      one in ten in the Civil War; one in forty-four of them died, against one in eleven. Four times
      as many men went and a quarter as many died, in a war a fifth as long. And where the Civil War
      roster gave a cause of death for forty-nine men and let this site tally Corinth, Atlanta and
      Andersonville, this list gives ninety-five names and nothing else — no unit, no date, no
      place. The influenza that killed eight per cent of the cases at the state hospital that same
      autumn cannot be told apart in it from the Argonne.
    </p>
    <p>
      The county's war itself had no battle in it and no local beginning the sources will give. The
      Civil War opened on a named Sunday at the courthouse with a flag strung on a wire. This one
      opens with committees: a draft board serving without pay, a Council of Defense chaired by the
      man who owned the truck works, a woman's branch that changed hands to avoid overlapping the
      Red Cross, and a War Savings drive that beat its $1,297,000 quota by $70,000 — after which,
      the book says, <em>the people did not incline to make a sacrifice again.</em> Men were sworn
      in at Memorial Hall, from a radius many miles in every direction, and given lunch. What the
      county built for the front was a truck, and the book captions it the first one used in the
      war.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is on the register, and what the register is</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={register} />
    <p>
      The National Park Service publishes the whole National Register as a map service, and this
      county's share of it is one request. Twenty-nine properties: twenty-six buildings, two
      structures and one historic district, from 15 October 1966 to 27 October 2004. Twenty-five
      are in Lima, two in Delphos, one in Shawnee Township and one in Spencer.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>
        Allen County on the National Register, by the date the Keeper signed each listing. The
        last column but one is not from the register: every point was put to the county's own map
        service, and all twenty-nine landed inside the county.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Listed</th><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Address</th><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Ref.</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
